/* Fun facts for the home page (Chemistry). Indonesian versions, in the same order, are in src/lang/id/facts.js. */
const FACTS = [
  { t: `Graphene was made with sticky tape`, b: `In 2004 Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov at the University of Manchester used ordinary sticky tape to peel thinner and thinner flakes from a lump of graphite, until they had graphene, a sheet of carbon just one atom thick. It is about 200 times stronger than steel and conducts electricity extremely well. They won the 2010 Nobel Prize in Physics.` },
  { t: `A Nobel Prize and an Ig Nobel Prize`, b: `Andre Geim, co-discoverer of graphene, is the only person to have won both a Nobel Prize (2010) and an Ig Nobel Prize, which honours research that "makes people laugh, then think". His Ig Nobel came in 2000 for using a very strong magnetic field to levitate a live frog, which works because water in the frog is weakly repelled by magnets.` },
  { t: `Mendeleev left gaps and was proved right`, b: `When Dmitri Mendeleev published his periodic table in 1869, he left gaps and predicted the properties of elements not yet discovered. Gallium (1875), scandium (1879) and germanium (1886) were soon found with almost exactly the atomic masses, densities and compounds he had forecast. Element 101, mendelevium, is named after him.` },
  { t: `The newest elements`, b: `The periodic table now has 118 confirmed elements. The four newest were named in 2016: nihonium (113, after Japan, Nihon), moscovium (115, after Moscow), tennessine (117, after Tennessee) and oganesson (118). They were made by smashing lighter nuclei together, and only a handful of atoms, each lasting a fraction of a second, have ever existed.` },
  { t: `An element named after a living scientist`, b: `Oganesson, element 118, is named after the Russian physicist Yuri Oganessian, who led the teams that made many superheavy elements. He is only the second person to have an element named after him while still alive; the first was Glenn Seaborg, honoured by seaborgium (106) in 1997.` },
  { t: `Helium was found in the Sun before the Earth`, b: `During a solar eclipse in 1868, astronomers saw a bright yellow line in the spectrum of the Sun that matched no known element. Norman Lockyer named the new element helium, from the Greek helios, "sun". It was not found on Earth until 1895, when William Ramsay detected it in gas released from a uranium mineral.` },
  { t: `Only two elements are liquid at room temperature`, b: `At normal room temperature, only mercury and bromine are liquids. But gallium melts at 29.8 °C, so it turns to liquid in your hand, and a gallium spoon will melt in a cup of hot tea. Caesium melts at about 28 °C, but it reacts so violently with air and water that it must be kept sealed.` },
  { t: `Diamonds can burn`, b: `Diamond is pure carbon, just like graphite and soot. In 1772 Antoine Lavoisier focused sunlight with a giant lens onto a diamond in a sealed jar and showed that it burns and produces carbon dioxide. At ordinary pressure diamond is actually slightly less stable than graphite, but the change is so slow that "diamonds are forever" is safe in practice.` },
  { t: `The father of modern chemistry lost his head`, b: `Antoine Lavoisier showed that mass is conserved in chemical reactions, named oxygen and hydrogen, and helped create the modern system of chemical names. He was also a tax collector, and during the French Revolution he was guillotined in 1794. The mathematician Lagrange said: "It took them only an instant to cut off that head, and a hundred years may not produce another like it."` },
  { t: `Oxygen's name is a mistake`, b: `Lavoisier named oxygen from Greek words meaning "acid maker", because he believed every acid contains oxygen. He was wrong: hydrochloric acid, HCl, has none. The element itself was discovered independently by Carl Wilhelm Scheele around 1772 and Joseph Priestley in 1774, before Lavoisier explained its role in burning and breathing.` },
  { t: `Hydrogen is most of the universe`, b: `Hydrogen, the simplest element, makes up about three quarters of all the ordinary matter in the universe by mass, and helium most of the rest. Everything else, including the carbon, oxygen and iron in your body, adds up to only about 2%. On Earth, most hydrogen is locked up in water.` },
  { t: `You are made of star dust`, b: `Almost every element in your body heavier than hydrogen and helium, the carbon in your cells, the oxygen you breathe, the calcium in your bones and the iron in your blood, was forged inside stars that lived and died before the Sun formed. Their explosions scattered these elements into the gas clouds that later made our Solar System.` },
  { t: `Gold comes from colliding neutron stars`, b: `Much of the gold, platinum and other heavy elements in the universe is thought to be made when two neutron stars collide. In 2017 astronomers watched such a collision, first detected as gravitational waves, and saw the glow of freshly made heavy elements, with an estimated several Earth masses of gold and platinum.` },
  { t: `All the gold ever mined would fit in a small cube`, b: `Around 216,000 tonnes of gold have been mined in all of human history. Gold is so dense (19.3 g/cm³) that all of it would fit in a cube only about 22 metres on each side, roughly the height of a seven-storey building. Gold does not rust or tarnish, so most of it is still in use today as jewellery, bars and electronics.` },
  { t: `Aluminium was once more precious than gold`, b: `Aluminium is the most common metal in the Earth's crust, but it is so tightly bound in its ores that it was very hard to extract. In the 1850s it cost more than gold, and Napoleon III reportedly served his most honoured guests on aluminium plates. In 1886 Charles Hall and Paul Héroult independently invented cheap electrolysis, and aluminium became an everyday metal.` },
  { t: `Marie Curie, twice a Nobel laureate`, b: `Marie Curie discovered polonium, named after her homeland Poland, and radium. She is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences: physics in 1903 and chemistry in 1911. Her notebooks are still so radioactive that they are kept in lead-lined boxes in Paris, and readers must wear protective clothing.` },
  { t: `More molecules in a glass than glasses in the sea`, b: `A glass of water (250 g) contains about 8 × 10²⁴ water molecules. All the oceans together hold roughly 1.3 billion cubic kilometres of water, about 5 × 10²¹ glassfuls. So there are over a thousand times more molecules in one glass of water than there are glasses of water in all the oceans on Earth.` },
  { t: `Avogadro's number is now exact`, b: `Since 20 May 2019 the Avogadro constant has been fixed at exactly 6.02214076 × 10²³ per mole, and the mole is defined as exactly that many particles. Chemists celebrate Mole Day on 23 October from 6:02 in the morning to 6:02 in the evening, because of the date and time 6:02 10/23.` },
  { t: `Most of your body is oxygen`, b: `By mass, your body is about 65% oxygen, 18% carbon, 10% hydrogen and 3% nitrogen, with calcium, phosphorus and a sprinkling of other elements making up the rest. The oxygen and hydrogen are mostly in water, which makes up around 60% of an adult's body. Yet by number of atoms, hydrogen is by far the most common.` },
  { t: `Argon is more common than carbon dioxide`, b: `Dry air is about 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen and 0.93% argon. Carbon dioxide, which matters so much for climate, is only about 0.04%. Argon, a noble gas, was discovered in 1894 by Lord Rayleigh and William Ramsay; its name comes from the Greek for "lazy", because it hardly reacts with anything.` },
  { t: `Neon signs are only red-orange`, b: `Pure neon glows red-orange when electricity passes through it. The other colours of "neon" signs come from different gases, such as argon with a little mercury for blue, or from coloured phosphor coatings inside the glass. Neon was discovered in 1898 by William Ramsay and Morris Travers, and its name means "new".` },
  { t: `Bread from air`, b: `The Haber–Bosch process, developed in Germany around 1909–1913, combines nitrogen from the air with hydrogen to make ammonia for fertiliser. It is estimated that about half of the world's population today is fed by crops grown with this synthetic nitrogen. Fritz Haber received the 1918 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, though he is also remembered for developing poison gas in the First World War.` },
  { t: `Teflon was an accident`, b: `In 1938 the chemist Roy Plunkett at DuPont opened a cylinder of a refrigerant gas and found that nothing came out, even though it still weighed the same. The gas had turned into a slippery white solid: polytetrafluoroethylene, PTFE, better known as Teflon. It is so unreactive and slippery that it is used for non-stick pans, and almost nothing sticks to it.` },
  { t: `The first fully synthetic plastic`, b: `In 1907 Leo Baekeland made Bakelite by reacting phenol with formaldehyde. It was the first fully synthetic plastic: hard, heat-resistant and a good electrical insulator, perfect for telephones, radios and light switches. Today the world produces more than 400 million tonnes of plastic every year.` },
  { t: `Nylon stockings caused queues`, b: `Wallace Carothers and his team at DuPont made nylon in 1935, the first synthetic fibre made entirely from chemicals. When nylon stockings went on sale in the USA in 1940, millions of pairs sold in days. During the Second World War nylon was needed for parachutes and ropes, and stockings became so scarce that there were "nylon riots" when they returned.` },
  { t: `Kevlar: stronger than steel`, b: `In 1965 the chemist Stephanie Kwolek at DuPont made a cloudy, runny polymer solution that colleagues wanted to throw away. She insisted on spinning it into fibres, and the result was Kevlar, about five times stronger than steel for the same weight. It is now used in bulletproof vests, helmets, ropes and bicycle tyres.` },
  { t: `Super glue was rejected for being too sticky`, b: `In 1942 Harry Coover was trying to make clear plastic gun sights and found that cyanoacrylates stuck to everything, so the project dropped them. Years later he realised the stickiness was the point, and super glue went on sale in 1958. It sets when traces of water on surfaces start the polymerisation, and it was even used to close wounds.` },
  { t: `A purple accident started the dye industry`, b: `In 1856 the 18-year-old William Perkin was trying to make the malaria drug quinine from coal tar. Instead he got a black sludge that dissolved to give a brilliant purple. He patented it as "mauveine", the first synthetic dye, and made purple, once a colour only the rich could afford, fashionable across Europe.` },
  { t: `Saccharin was found by unwashed hands`, b: `In 1879 Constantin Fahlberg, working on coal-tar chemicals, noticed that his bread tasted remarkably sweet at dinner. He had not washed his hands after work in the lab, and he traced the sweetness back to the compound saccharin, about 300–400 times sweeter than sugar. Tasting chemicals in the lab is extremely dangerous, so do not try this.` },
  { t: `Dynamite and the Nobel Prizes`, b: `Alfred Nobel invented dynamite in 1867 by soaking unstable nitroglycerine into a porous powdery earth called kieselguhr, making it safe to handle. In 1888 a newspaper mistakenly printed his obituary, calling him "the merchant of death". Shocked, Nobel left most of his fortune to fund the prizes that bear his name, first awarded in 1901.` },
  { t: `One village, four elements`, b: `A quarry near the village of Ytterby in Sweden produced minerals from which chemists isolated several new elements in the 19th century. Four of them are named after the village: yttrium, ytterbium, terbium and erbium. No other place on Earth has given its name to so many elements.` },
  { t: `The metal with the highest melting point`, b: `Tungsten melts at 3,422 °C, the highest melting point of any metal, which is why it was used for the glowing filaments of old light bulbs. Its symbol W comes from its other name, wolfram. The densest element of all is osmium, at about 22.6 g/cm³: a litre of it would weigh over 22 kg.` },
  { t: `The "fluorine martyrs"`, b: `Fluorine is the most reactive element, and for decades chemists who tried to isolate it were poisoned or injured; several died. In 1886 Henri Moissan finally made it by electrolysis at low temperature in equipment made of platinum, and he won the 1906 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Tiny amounts of fluoride in toothpaste help harden tooth enamel.` },
  { t: `Astronauts drank water from their fuel cells`, b: `The Apollo spacecraft got their electricity from hydrogen–oxygen fuel cells. The only waste product was pure water, which the astronauts used for drinking. Fuel cells make electricity directly from a chemical reaction, without burning, and today they power some buses, cars and backup generators.` },
  { t: `Octopuses have blue blood`, b: `Human blood is red because haemoglobin carries oxygen using iron. Octopuses, squid and horseshoe crabs use haemocyanin instead, which contains copper and turns blue when it carries oxygen. Octopuses also have three hearts. In plants, chlorophyll has a magnesium atom at its centre, in a ring structure very similar to the one in haemoglobin.` },
  { t: `Bananas are slightly radioactive`, b: `Bananas are rich in potassium, and a tiny fraction of natural potassium is radioactive potassium-40. Eating a banana gives you a very small dose of radiation, so small that the "banana equivalent dose" is sometimes used to explain radiation levels. Your own body contains about 4,000 decaying potassium-40 atoms every second.` },
  { t: `Carbon-14 tells the age of ancient things`, b: `Living things constantly take in carbon, including a small, steady fraction of radioactive carbon-14. When they die, the carbon-14 decays with a half-life of about 5,730 years and is not replaced. By measuring how much is left, Willard Libby showed in 1949 how to date wood, bone and cloth up to about 50,000 years old, and won the 1960 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.` },
  { t: `From sand to smartphones`, b: `Computer chips are made from silicon, the second most common element in the Earth's crust after oxygen, found in ordinary sand as silicon dioxide. For chips it must be purified to about 99.9999999% or better, so that fewer than one atom in a billion is an impurity. Tiny, controlled amounts of boron or phosphorus are then added to make the transistors work.` },
  { t: `The smell of rain has a name`, b: `The earthy smell after rain on dry ground is called petrichor, a word coined by two Australian scientists in 1964. Much of it comes from geosmin, a compound made by soil bacteria. The human nose is extraordinarily sensitive to geosmin and can detect it at a few parts per trillion, more sensitive than a shark is to blood.` },
  { t: `Why onions make you cry`, b: `When you cut an onion, you break its cells and mix enzymes with sulfur compounds. The reaction releases a volatile gas, syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which reaches your eyes and reacts with the water there to irritate them, so your eyes make tears to wash it away. Chilling the onion first slows the reaction.` },
  { t: `Measuring the heat of chilli`, b: `The heat of chillies comes from capsaicin, which triggers the same nerve receptors in your mouth that detect burning heat. In 1912 the pharmacist Wilbur Scoville created a scale by diluting chilli extract until tasters could no longer feel it. Capsaicin dissolves in fat and alcohol but not in water, which is why milk soothes a burning mouth better than water.` },
  { t: `Why durian smells so strong`, b: `Chemists who analysed durian in 2012 identified about 50 compounds that contribute to its aroma, including fruity esters and pungent sulfur compounds similar to those in onions and rotten eggs. The mixture is so powerful that durian is banned on public transport and in many hotels in Singapore and other parts of Southeast Asia.` },
  { t: `Chocolate is designed to melt in your mouth`, b: `Cocoa butter can crystallise in six different forms, each with its own melting point. Chocolate makers carefully "temper" chocolate so it sets in form V, which melts at about 34 °C: solid and snappy at room temperature, but melting just below body temperature on your tongue. If chocolate gets warm and re-sets in the wrong form, a whitish "bloom" appears.` },
  { t: `The chemistry of firework colours`, b: `Firework colours come from metal salts heated in the explosion: strontium gives red, sodium yellow, barium green, copper blue and a mix of strontium and copper purple. Each metal's electrons jump to higher energy levels and emit light of particular wavelengths as they fall back. A deep, pure blue is the hardest colour for pyrotechnicians to make.` },
  { t: `The Statue of Liberty used to be copper-coloured`, b: `The Statue of Liberty has a skin of copper sheets only about 2.4 mm thick. When it was unveiled in 1886 it was shiny reddish-brown. Over the following decades, the copper reacted with air, water and pollutants to form a green patina of copper carbonates, sulfates and chlorides, which now protects the metal underneath from further corrosion.` },
  { t: `Why stainless steel does not rust`, b: `In 1913 Harry Brearley in Sheffield found that steel containing about 13% chromium did not rust. The chromium reacts with oxygen to form an invisible layer of chromium oxide only a few nanometres thick, which seals the surface. If the layer is scratched, it repairs itself almost instantly in air.` },
  { t: `Dry ice skips the liquid stage`, b: `Solid carbon dioxide, "dry ice", is at −78.5 °C and turns directly from solid to gas without melting, which is called sublimation. That is why it leaves no puddle and is used to ship frozen food and vaccines. The fog effect on stages comes from dropping dry ice into warm water: the cold gas condenses water vapour into tiny droplets.` },
  { t: `Liquid nitrogen boils at −196 °C`, b: `Nitrogen, which makes up 78% of the air, becomes liquid only below −196 °C. Poured onto a warm floor, it boils so fast that the drops skate around on a cushion of vapour. It is used to freeze and store cells and embryos, to remove warts, and to make ice cream in seconds; because it expands about 700 times as it boils, it must never be sealed in a closed container.` },
  { t: `A bacterium that eats plastic bottles`, b: `PET plastic bottles can last for centuries in the environment. In 2016 Japanese scientists reported a bacterium, Ideonella sakaiensis, found near a bottle recycling plant, that can break down PET using two enzymes and live on it. Researchers are now engineering faster versions of these enzymes to recycle plastic back into its building blocks.` },
  { t: `Enzymes are astonishingly fast`, b: `Enzymes are protein catalysts. One of the most impressive, orotidine 5'-phosphate decarboxylase, speeds up a reaction needed to make DNA and RNA by a factor of about 10¹⁷. Without it, the reaction would have a half-life of about 78 million years; with the enzyme, it happens in a few milliseconds.` },
  { t: `Catalytic converters use precious metals`, b: `A car's catalytic converter contains tiny amounts of platinum, palladium and rhodium spread on a honeycomb of ceramic. They turn toxic carbon monoxide, nitrogen oxides and unburnt fuel into carbon dioxide, nitrogen and water. Rhodium is so rare that in 2021 it became the most expensive precious metal, worth many times the price of gold.` },
  { t: `Indonesia leads the world in nickel`, b: `Indonesia is the world's largest producer of nickel, supplying about half of global output in recent years. Nickel is used in stainless steel and, increasingly, in the cathodes of lithium-ion batteries for electric vehicles. Much of Indonesia's nickel ore comes from Sulawesi and Halmahera, where it is refined in large industrial parks.` },
  { t: `The islands of tin`, b: `The islands of Bangka and Belitung have been mined for tin for centuries, making Indonesia one of the world's top tin producers. Tin is essential for solder, which joins components on almost every circuit board in phones and computers. It is also used to coat steel cans, because tin resists corrosion.` },
  { t: `Your stomach holds hydrochloric acid`, b: `Gastric acid in your stomach has a pH of about 1.5 to 3.5, strong enough to dissolve some metals. It kills most bacteria in food and helps the enzyme pepsin break down proteins. The stomach protects itself with a thick layer of mucus and by constantly renewing its lining cells.` },
  { t: `Pure water hardly conducts electricity`, b: `It is the dissolved ions in tap water, sea water and sweat, not the water molecules themselves, that let electricity flow. Ultrapure water used in chip factories conducts electricity about a million times less well than sea water. Even so, never use electrical devices near water: ordinary water always contains enough ions to be dangerous.` },
  { t: `A dangerous metal plus a poisonous gas makes salt`, b: `Sodium is a soft metal that reacts violently with water, and chlorine is a toxic green gas that was used as a weapon in the First World War. Yet when they react together they form sodium chloride, ordinary table salt, which we need to live. Compounds can have completely different properties from the elements they are made of.` },
  { t: `Water is densest at 4 °C`, b: `Most liquids get denser as they cool, but water is densest at about 4 °C and expands as it approaches freezing. Hydrogen bonds hold the molecules of ice in an open hexagonal lattice with lots of empty space, which is why ice floats and why snowflakes have six-fold symmetry.` },
  { t: `Why ozone smells after a storm`, b: `Lightning splits oxygen molecules, and some of the atoms join O₂ to form ozone, O₃, which has a sharp, clean smell; its name comes from the Greek for "to smell". High in the stratosphere, the ozone layer absorbs most of the Sun's harmful ultraviolet light. Near the ground, however, ozone is a pollutant that irritates the lungs.` },
  { t: `The rarest natural element`, b: `Astatine, element 85, is formed only briefly during the decay of uranium and thorium, and its longest-lived form has a half-life of about 8 hours. It is estimated that less than a gram of it exists in the entire Earth's crust at any moment. No one has ever seen a visible piece of it, because any sample would vaporise from its own radioactive heat.` },
  { t: `Vulcanised rubber was discovered on a stove`, b: `Natural rubber turns sticky in heat and brittle in cold. In 1839 Charles Goodyear accidentally dropped a mixture of rubber and sulfur on a hot stove and found it became tough and elastic. Sulfur atoms form cross-links between the long rubber molecules. Vulcanised rubber made car tyres possible, and Indonesia is today one of the world's largest producers of natural rubber.` },
  { t: `The symbol J is missing`, b: `Look at the periodic table and you will not find the letter J in any element symbol. Symbols come from names in Latin, Greek, German or other languages: Fe for iron (ferrum), Na for sodium (natrium), K for potassium (kalium), Pb for lead (plumbum), Au for gold (aurum) and Ag for silver (argentum).` },
  { t: `Glass is an amorphous solid`, b: `In a crystal such as quartz, atoms sit in a regular repeating pattern. Glass is made of the same main ingredient, silicon dioxide, but it is cooled so quickly from the melt that the atoms freeze in a disordered arrangement, like a snapshot of a liquid. Nature makes glass too: obsidian forms when lava cools very fast.` },
  { t: `Rust is slow burning`, b: `Rusting and burning are both oxidation reactions: iron combining with oxygen releases heat, just much more slowly. Hand-warmer packets use this: they contain iron powder, salt and water, and when you open them and let air in, the iron rusts quickly enough to warm your hands for hours.` },
  { t: `Why soap removes grease`, b: `A soap molecule has a water-loving head and a long oil-loving tail. In water, the tails bury themselves in grease while the heads face outwards, forming tiny balls called micelles that can be rinsed away. People have made soap for more than 4,000 years by heating fats with alkali from wood ash; the Babylonians already recorded a recipe.` },
  { t: `Water is a rare liquid`, b: `Water molecules are tiny (H₂O has a molar mass of only 18 g/mol), and similar small molecules such as hydrogen sulfide (H₂S) are gases at room temperature. Water stays liquid up to 100 °C because of strong hydrogen bonds between its molecules. Without them, the oceans would boil away and life as we know it could not exist.` },
  { t: `The periodic table has a rival shape`, b: `Mendeleev's table is not the only way to arrange the elements. Chemists have drawn spirals, pyramids, three-dimensional shapes and even Theodor Benfey's "snail" layout of 1964. The familiar long rectangular form is used because it groups elements with similar properties into columns and matches the filling of electron orbitals.` },
  { t: `Carbon is in most known compounds`, b: `Chemists have described over 100 million different compounds, and the great majority contain carbon. Carbon can form four strong bonds, link to itself in long chains and rings, and bond with hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and many others. That is why a whole branch, organic chemistry, is devoted to it.` },
  { t: `Urea ended the idea of a "life force"`, b: `In 1828 Friedrich Wöhler made urea, a substance found in urine, by heating ammonium cyanate in his laboratory. Until then many believed organic compounds could only be made by living things through a special "vital force". Wöhler wrote that he could make urea "without needing kidneys". Today urea is one of the most widely used fertilisers in Indonesia.` },
  { t: `Kekulé's dream of a snake`, b: `August Kekulé said the ring structure of benzene came to him in a daydream in which a snake seized its own tail. He proposed the six-membered ring in 1865. Whether or not the story is true, it is one of the most famous tales in chemistry, and benzene rings are now found in medicines, dyes and plastics.` },
  { t: `Aspirin comes from willow bark`, b: `For thousands of years people chewed willow bark to ease pain and fever. Its active ingredient, salicin, is turned into salicylic acid in the body, which irritates the stomach. In 1897 Felix Hoffmann at Bayer made a gentler form, acetylsalicylic acid, sold as Aspirin from 1899. It is still one of the most used medicines in the world.` },
  { t: `Penicillin grew on a forgotten dish`, b: `In 1928 Alexander Fleming returned from holiday to find mould growing on a bacteria culture, with a clear ring where the bacteria had died. The mould made penicillin. It took Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and a large team in the 1940s to purify and mass-produce it, and the three shared the 1945 Nobel Prize. Antibiotics have since saved hundreds of millions of lives.` },
  { t: `The medicine made from sweet wormwood`, b: `Tu Youyou, a Chinese scientist, searched ancient Chinese medical texts for a malaria remedy and found a 1,600-year-old recipe using sweet wormwood. Realising that heat destroyed the active ingredient, she extracted it at low temperature, discovering artemisinin in 1972. It has saved millions of lives, and she won the 2015 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.` },
  { t: `Quinine and the gin and tonic`, b: `Quinine, from the bark of the South American cinchona tree, was the main malaria drug for three centuries. In the 19th century the Dutch grew cinchona on plantations in Java, which became the world's main source of quinine until the Second World War. British officers in India mixed bitter quinine water with gin, creating the gin and tonic.` },
  { t: `The smell of gas is added on purpose`, b: `Natural gas (mostly methane) and LPG have no smell, so a leak would be undetectable. Suppliers add tiny amounts of strongly smelling sulfur compounds called mercaptans, which the human nose can detect at a few parts per billion. That "gas smell" is a safety feature: if you smell it, open the windows and do not switch on anything electrical.` },
  { t: `Carbon monoxide: the silent killer`, b: `Carbon monoxide (CO), produced when fuel burns without enough oxygen, binds to haemoglobin about 200 to 250 times more strongly than oxygen, so blood can no longer carry oxygen. It has no colour or smell. Running a generator or charcoal stove indoors, or sitting in a car with the engine running in a closed garage, can be fatal.` },
  { t: `Why cut apples turn brown`, b: `When an apple or banana is cut, an enzyme called polyphenol oxidase meets oxygen and turns colourless compounds into brown pigments. Lemon juice slows the reaction because its acid lowers the pH and vitamin C reacts with oxygen first. Cold also slows the enzyme, which is why chilled fruit browns more slowly.` },
  { t: `The chemistry of browning food`, b: `The delicious brown crust on fried tempe, grilled satay and toast comes from the Maillard reaction between sugars and amino acids above about 140 °C, named after Louis-Camille Maillard, who described it in 1912. It creates hundreds of flavour and aroma compounds. Caramelisation, the browning of sugar alone, is a different reaction.` },
  { t: `Tempe is a chemistry lab`, b: `Making tempe is controlled fermentation: the mould Rhizopus grows through cooked soybeans in about 36 to 48 hours, binding them into a firm cake. Its enzymes break down proteins and fats into more digestible pieces and reduce some compounds that block mineral absorption. Tempe originated in Java and is now studied by food scientists worldwide.` },
  { t: `Why bread rises`, b: `Yeast feeds on sugars in dough and produces carbon dioxide gas and a little alcohol. The gluten proteins in wheat flour form a stretchy network that traps the gas bubbles, so the dough rises. In the oven the gas expands further, the alcohol evaporates and the structure sets. Baking powder does a similar job chemically, using an acid and sodium bicarbonate.` },
  { t: `Soda bubbles come from pressure`, b: `Soft drinks are bottled with carbon dioxide dissolved under pressure. When you open the bottle the pressure drops, and CO₂ comes out of solution as bubbles, as described by Henry's law. Cold drinks hold more gas, which is why warm soda goes flat faster. Dropping a candy with a rough surface into cola gives the gas many places to form bubbles at once, causing the famous fountain.` },
  { t: `Salt melts ice`, b: `Adding salt to ice lowers the freezing point of water, a colligative property, so the ice melts even below 0 °C. That is why roads are salted in cold countries. The melting absorbs heat, making the salty slush colder: a mixture of ice and salt can reach about −21 °C, cold enough to make ice cream in a bucket without a freezer.` },
  { t: `Instant cold packs are endothermic`, b: `Sports cold packs contain water and a pouch of ammonium nitrate or urea. When you squeeze the pack, the pouch bursts and the solid dissolves, absorbing heat from its surroundings, so the pack quickly feels icy. Self-heating cans work the other way, using an exothermic reaction such as quicklime with water.` },
  { t: `Glow sticks are cold light`, b: `Bending a glow stick breaks a glass vial inside, mixing hydrogen peroxide with a phenyl oxalate ester and a fluorescent dye. The reaction releases energy that excites the dye, which emits light without getting hot: chemiluminescence. Putting a glow stick in the freezer slows the reaction, so it glows more dimly but lasts longer.` },
  { t: `Fireflies make light with chemistry`, b: `Fireflies produce light when the enzyme luciferase makes the molecule luciferin react with oxygen and ATP. Almost all of the energy comes out as light, not heat, making it one of the most efficient light sources known. Scientists use the firefly luciferase gene in the lab as a glowing marker to track which genes are switched on.` },
  { t: `The green glow from a jellyfish`, b: `In 1962 Osamu Shimomura isolated green fluorescent protein (GFP) from a glowing jellyfish. Later scientists learned to attach the GFP gene to other genes so that cells light up green under blue light, letting researchers watch proteins inside living cells. Shimomura, Martin Chalfie and Roger Tsien shared the 2008 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.` },
  { t: `Lithium-ion batteries changed the world`, b: `The rechargeable lithium-ion battery, developed by John Goodenough, M. Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino and first sold in 1991, powers phones, laptops and electric cars. Lithium is the lightest metal, so it stores a lot of energy for its weight. The three shared the 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry; Goodenough, at 97, became the oldest Nobel laureate ever.` },
  { t: `Volta's pile, the first battery`, b: `In 1800 Alessandro Volta stacked discs of zinc and copper separated by cloth soaked in salt water and produced a steady electric current: the first battery. It started from an argument with Luigi Galvani about why dissected frog legs twitched. The unit of voltage, the volt, is named after Volta.` },
  { t: `Humphry Davy discovered six elements with electricity`, b: `Using large batteries, Humphry Davy passed electricity through molten salts and in 1807–1808 isolated potassium, sodium, calcium, magnesium, barium and strontium. When potassium first appeared as tiny drops that burst into flame, he reportedly danced around the laboratory. His assistant Michael Faraday became even more famous.` },
  { t: `Phosphorus was discovered in urine`, b: `In 1669 the German alchemist Hennig Brand, searching for the philosopher's stone, boiled down large quantities of urine and obtained a white substance that glowed in the dark. It was phosphorus, the first element discovered by a known person. Its name means "light-bearer" in Greek. White phosphorus glows because it slowly reacts with oxygen in the air.` },
  { t: `Iodised salt prevents goitre`, b: `Iodine is needed for the thyroid gland, and a lack of it causes goitre and can harm a child's brain development. Since the 1920s many countries add a little potassium iodate to table salt. Indonesia has required iodised salt for decades, which is why packets are labelled "garam beryodium".` },
  { t: `Fluoride in toothpaste hardens enamel`, b: `Tooth enamel is mostly hydroxyapatite, a calcium phosphate mineral. Fluoride ions replace some hydroxide ions to form fluorapatite, which resists acid from bacteria better. That is why most toothpastes contain about 1,000 to 1,500 ppm of fluoride, a very small but effective amount.` },
  { t: `Why rubbing alcohol feels cold`, b: `Alcohol on your skin evaporates quickly, and evaporation absorbs heat from your skin, so it feels cold. Sweat cools you the same way, although water evaporates more slowly. In humid weather, like much of Indonesia, sweat evaporates less, which is why hot, humid days feel so uncomfortable.` },
  { t: `Clay pots cool water`, b: `Unglazed clay pots such as the traditional kendi keep drinking water cool: water seeps through tiny pores and evaporates from the outer surface, taking heat with it. The same principle is used in the "pot-in-pot" cooler, where wet sand between two clay pots keeps vegetables fresh without electricity.` },
  { t: `Hard water and soap scum`, b: `Water that has passed through limestone contains dissolved calcium and magnesium ions: "hard water". These ions react with soap to form an insoluble grey scum and build up as limescale in kettles. Synthetic detergents were developed partly because they still work in hard water.` },
  { t: `Caves are built by chemistry`, b: `Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide and becomes weakly acidic, slowly dissolving limestone to form caves. Where the water drips from the ceiling it loses CO₂ and deposits calcium carbonate again, growing stalactites down and stalagmites up, often less than a few millimetres per year. Indonesia has spectacular karst caves such as Gua Jomblang in Yogyakarta and those in the Maros-Pangkep karst of South Sulawesi.` },
  { t: `The world's oldest cave paintings used chemistry`, b: `Hand stencils and animal paintings in caves of the Maros-Pangkep karst in Sulawesi are among the oldest known art: one painting of a Sulawesi warty pig is at least 45,500 years old, and a scene of people and a pig dated in 2024 is at least 51,200 years old. Scientists dated it by measuring uranium decaying into thorium in thin mineral crusts that formed over the paint.` },
  { t: `Chemistry of batik dyeing`, b: `Batik uses wax as a "resist": areas covered in melted wax (malam) do not absorb dye. Traditional colours came from plants such as indigo (Indigofera) for blue and soga bark for brown. Natural indigo is insoluble; dyers reduce it to a soluble yellowish form, and it turns blue again as it reacts with oxygen in the air after the cloth is lifted out.` },
  { t: `Why leaves change colour`, b: `Leaves are green because chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light and reflects green. Yellow and orange carotenoids are there all along, hidden by the chlorophyll. When trees in cold climates stop making chlorophyll in autumn, the carotenoids show through, and some trees also make red anthocyanins, the same pigments that colour purple sweet potatoes.` },
  { t: `Red cabbage is a pH indicator`, b: `The purple pigment in red cabbage and butterfly-pea flower (bunga telang) is an anthocyanin that changes colour with pH: red in acid, purple near neutral, and blue to green in alkali. Pour lime juice into blue butterfly-pea tea and it turns purple-pink, a popular trick in drinks, and a simple homemade acid–base indicator.` },
  { t: `Ants shoot formic acid`, b: `The sting and bite of many ants hurt because of formic acid, HCOOH, the simplest carboxylic acid. Its name comes from the Latin "formica", ant, because it was first obtained in 1671 by distilling crushed ants. Stinging nettles also inject formic acid along with other irritants.` },
  { t: `Snake venom is a protein cocktail`, b: `Most snake venoms are mixtures of dozens of proteins and enzymes that attack nerves, blood or tissue. Antivenom is made by injecting small, safe doses of venom into horses or sheep and collecting the antibodies their bodies produce. Indonesia, home to cobras, kraits and vipers, produces its own antivenom.` },
  { t: `Vitamin C and sailors' scurvy`, b: `Sailors on long voyages once died of scurvy, whose symptoms include bleeding gums. In 1747 the Scottish doctor James Lind ran one of the first controlled trials and found that citrus fruit cured it. The cause, a lack of vitamin C (ascorbic acid), was identified in the 1930s; humans, unlike most animals, cannot make it themselves.` },
  { t: `Rice, sago and starch`, b: `Starch, the main energy store in rice, corn, cassava and sago, is a polymer of thousands of glucose units. Amylose chains coil up while amylopectin branches; sticky rice (ketan) is almost pure amylopectin, which is why it is so sticky. Cooling cooked rice turns some starch into "resistant starch", which is digested more slowly.` },
  { t: `Palm oil is the world's most used vegetable oil`, b: `Palm oil, from the fruit of the oil palm, is the most produced vegetable oil in the world, and Indonesia is by far the largest producer. It is rich in saturated palmitic acid, which keeps it semi-solid at cool temperatures, useful for margarine and chocolate spreads. It is also turned into biodiesel by reacting it with methanol.` },
  { t: `Coconut oil and soap`, b: `Coconut oil is rich in lauric acid, a medium-length fatty acid. When fats react with a strong base such as sodium hydroxide, they form soap and glycerol, a reaction called saponification. Coconut oil soaps lather well even in cold or salty water, which is why they were long made in coastal regions.` },
  { t: `Silver is the best conductor`, b: `Of all metals, silver conducts electricity and heat the best, followed by copper and gold. Copper is used for most wiring because it is almost as good and much cheaper. Gold is used for tiny connections in electronics not because it conducts best but because it never corrodes, so contacts stay reliable for decades.` },
  { t: `Mercury is being phased out`, b: `Mercury was used in thermometers for centuries because it expands evenly with temperature. But its vapour is toxic, and the Minamata disaster in Japan in the 1950s, caused by factory waste containing methylmercury, poisoned thousands of people. The 2013 Minamata Convention, which Indonesia ratified, is phasing out mercury, including its use in small-scale gold mining.` },
  { t: `Lead in petrol was a global mistake`, b: `From the 1920s lead compounds were added to petrol to stop engine knocking, and lead from exhausts spread worldwide, harming children's brains. The chemist Clair Patterson, who measured the age of the Earth, also discovered how badly lead had polluted the environment and campaigned for a ban. Leaded petrol was phased out; Indonesia stopped selling it in 2006, and the last country ended it in 2021.` },
  { t: `The Earth is 4.54 billion years old`, b: `In 1956 Clair Patterson measured the ratios of lead isotopes in meteorites, which formed with the Earth, and calculated an age of about 4.55 billion years. Modern measurements give 4.54 ± 0.05 billion years. Radioactive uranium slowly decays to lead at a known rate, so it works like a natural clock.` },
  { t: `Cement is the most used material after water`, b: `Concrete is the most used human-made material on Earth, and cement is its glue. Cement is made by heating limestone and clay to about 1,450 °C; when mixed with water it forms interlocking crystals of calcium silicate hydrate. Making cement releases carbon dioxide, and the industry produces about 8% of global CO₂ emissions.` },
  { t: `Roman concrete heals itself`, b: `Some Roman harbour walls built 2,000 years ago are still standing in seawater. Researchers found that Roman concrete, made with volcanic ash and lime, grows new minerals when seawater seeps in, strengthening it over time. In 2023 MIT scientists showed that small lumps of lime in it also help cracks seal themselves when water gets in.` },
  { t: `Chemistry won the Nobel Prize for "click" reactions`, b: `Click chemistry, developed by K. Barry Sharpless and Morten Meldal, joins molecular building blocks quickly and cleanly, like snapping buckles together. Carolyn Bertozzi adapted it to work inside living cells without harming them. The three shared the 2022 Nobel Prize in Chemistry; it was Sharpless's second chemistry Nobel.` },
  { t: `Computers that predict protein shapes`, b: `Proteins fold into complex 3-D shapes that decide what they do, and working out a single shape by experiment could take years. In 2020 the AI system AlphaFold predicted protein structures almost as accurately as experiments. Demis Hassabis and John Jumper of Google DeepMind and David Baker, who designed new proteins, shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.` },
  { t: `Rosalind Franklin's Photo 51`, b: `In 1952 Rosalind Franklin and her student Raymond Gosling took an X-ray diffraction image of DNA, "Photo 51", whose X-shaped pattern revealed a helix. Seen without her knowledge, it helped James Watson and Francis Crick propose the double helix in 1953. Franklin died in 1958, four years before Watson, Crick and Maurice Wilkins received the Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `Linus Pauling won two unshared Nobel Prizes`, b: `Linus Pauling explained the nature of the chemical bond, introduced electronegativity and described the helical structures in proteins, winning the 1954 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. He later campaigned against nuclear weapons testing and won the 1962 Nobel Peace Prize. He is the only person to have won two unshared Nobel Prizes.` },
  { t: `Frederick Sanger won chemistry twice`, b: `Frederick Sanger is the only person to win the Nobel Prize in Chemistry twice: in 1958 for working out the amino acid sequence of insulin, the first protein sequenced, and in 1980 for a method of reading the sequence of DNA. The Sanger method was used to read much of the first human genome.` },
  { t: `Insulin: from dogs to bacteria`, b: `In 1921–1922 Frederick Banting and Charles Best extracted insulin from animal pancreases and saved the lives of children with diabetes; Banting and John Macleod received the 1923 Nobel Prize for it. For decades insulin came from cows and pigs. Since 1982, human insulin has been made by genetically modified bacteria, the first medicine produced this way.` },
  { t: `Ozone-destroying CFCs were once "miracle" chemicals`, b: `Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), introduced in the 1930s, were non-toxic and non-flammable, perfect for fridges and spray cans. In 1974 Mario Molina and Sherwood Rowland showed that in the stratosphere, UV light releases chlorine atoms from CFCs, and each chlorine atom can destroy tens of thousands of ozone molecules. With Paul Crutzen they won the 1995 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.` },
  { t: `Measuring pH started with beer`, b: `The pH scale was introduced in 1909 by the Danish chemist Søren Sørensen, who worked at the Carlsberg brewery laboratory studying how acidity affects enzymes. Instead of writing concentrations like 0.0000001 mol/L, he used the negative logarithm, giving tidy numbers from 0 to 14 that everyone now learns at school.` },
  { t: `The strongest acid ever made`, b: `Chemists call acids stronger than 100% sulfuric acid "superacids". Fluoroantimonic acid, made from hydrogen fluoride and antimony pentafluoride, is estimated to be up to 10¹⁶ times stronger than pure sulfuric acid. It must be stored in Teflon containers because it attacks glass. George Olah won the 1994 Nobel Prize for using superacids to study carbon ions.` },
  { t: `Aerogel, the lightest solid`, b: `Silica aerogel is a solid made mostly of air: up to about 99.8% of its volume is empty space. It is so light it looks like "frozen smoke", yet it is an excellent insulator; a thin layer can protect a flower from a flame. NASA used aerogel on the Stardust probe to catch comet dust gently in 2004.` },
  { t: `Metals that remember their shape`, b: `Nitinol, an alloy of nickel and titanium, can be bent out of shape and then return to its original form when warmed, because its crystal structure switches between two arrangements. This "shape memory" is used in heart stents that expand inside blood vessels, flexible spectacle frames and orthodontic wires.` },
  { t: `Democritus imagined atoms 2,400 years ago`, b: `Around 400 BCE the Greek thinker Democritus argued that matter cannot be cut forever: eventually you reach tiny pieces that cannot be divided, which he called atomos, "uncuttable". He had no experiments to prove it. The idea was revived with evidence by John Dalton in 1808.` },
  { t: `Dalton was colour-blind`, b: `John Dalton, who founded modern atomic theory, was also the first to describe colour blindness scientifically, in 1794, based on his own vision. He asked for his eyes to be examined after his death. In 1995 DNA from his preserved eye showed he lacked the green-sensitive pigment. Red–green colour blindness was long called "Daltonism".` },
  { t: `Alchemists wanted to make gold`, b: `For centuries alchemists tried to turn lead into gold and searched for an elixir of life. They failed, but they invented distillation equipment, discovered acids and developed careful laboratory methods. Isaac Newton wrote over a million words on alchemy. In 1980 physicists did turn a tiny amount of bismuth into gold using a particle accelerator, at enormous cost.` },
  { t: `Jabir ibn Hayyan and the chemistry lab`, b: `Writings attributed to Jabir ibn Hayyan, from the 8th–10th centuries in the Islamic world, describe distillation, crystallisation and the preparation of acids. Many chemistry words came to English through Arabic, including alchemy, alcohol, alkali and elixir.` },
  { t: `Robert Boyle doubted the four elements`, b: `Ancient thinkers said everything was made of earth, water, air and fire. In 1661 Robert Boyle's book The Sceptical Chymist argued that elements should be found by experiment, not argument. He also discovered that the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume, now called Boyle's law.` },
  { t: `The phlogiston mistake`, b: `In the 1700s chemists thought burning released a substance called phlogiston. But metals gain weight when they burn, so phlogiston would need negative weight. Antoine Lavoisier showed in the 1770s that burning is combining with oxygen, and by carefully weighing everything he established that mass is conserved in reactions.` },
  { t: `Marie-Anne Lavoisier, the silent partner`, b: `Marie-Anne Paulze Lavoisier worked alongside her husband Antoine: she translated English chemistry papers, kept laboratory records and drew precise illustrations of their equipment for his 1789 textbook. After his execution in 1794 she arranged publication of his memoirs. Historians now see her as a key contributor to the chemical revolution.` },
  { t: `Laughing gas parties`, b: `In 1799 the young Humphry Davy experimented on himself by breathing nitrous oxide and found it made him laugh and feel euphoric. "Laughing gas" parties became fashionable. Decades later, in the 1840s, dentists began using it as an anaesthetic, and it is still used today.` },
  { t: `Bunsen's burner and the spectroscope`, b: `Robert Bunsen's burner, developed in 1855 with Peter Desaga, gave a hot, almost colourless flame. With Gustav Kirchhoff, Bunsen used it to study the coloured light from heated elements through a prism. Every element produced its own set of lines, and they discovered caesium (1860) and rubidium (1861) this way.` },
  { t: `Caesium keeps the world's time`, b: `Since 1967 the second has been defined by caesium atoms: it is exactly 9,192,631,770 oscillations of the microwave radiation that makes a caesium-133 atom switch between two energy states. Caesium atomic clocks in laboratories around the world keep official time and help GPS work.` },
  { t: `Noble gases were thought unable to react`, b: `The noble gases were long believed to form no compounds at all. In 1962 Neil Bartlett mixed xenon with platinum hexafluoride and made an orange-yellow solid: the first noble gas compound. Since then many xenon and krypton compounds have been made, though helium and neon still form no stable neutral compounds under ordinary conditions.` },
  { t: `The periodic table's many names`, b: `Element names come from many sources: planets (uranium, neptunium, plutonium), places (germanium, polonium, francium, americium), scientists (einsteinium, curium, mendelevium), myths (titanium, promethium) and colours (chlorine from Greek for pale green, iridium from "rainbow").` },
  { t: `Cobalt and nickel were named after goblins`, b: `German miners found ores that looked valuable but gave off poisonous arsenic fumes and yielded no copper. They blamed mischievous spirits: "kobold" (goblin) and "Nickel" (a demon, from "Kupfernickel", devil's copper). The metals later isolated from these ores were named cobalt and nickel.` },
  { t: `Seaweed gave us iodine`, b: `In 1811 the French chemist Bernard Courtois was making saltpetre for gunpowder from seaweed ash when he added too much sulfuric acid. Beautiful violet vapour rose and condensed into dark crystals: a new element, named iodine from the Greek for "violet". Seaweed is still a rich source of iodine.` },
  { t: `Bromine is named for its smell`, b: `Bromine, discovered in 1826 by Antoine Balard in the salt marshes of Montpellier, is one of only two elements that are liquid at room temperature. It is a dark red-brown liquid with a choking smell, and its name comes from the Greek "bromos", meaning stench.` },
  { t: `Tungsten is named "heavy stone"`, b: `Tungsten comes from Swedish "tung sten", meaning heavy stone, but its symbol W comes from its German name, Wolfram. Its density, 19.3 g/cm³, is almost exactly the same as gold's, which has led criminals to make fake gold bars with tungsten cores.` },
  { t: `Mendeleev's playing cards`, b: `Legend says Dmitri Mendeleev wrote each element on a card and played "chemical solitaire", arranging them until the pattern appeared, and that the final table came to him in a dream in 1869. The dream story may be exaggerated, but his card-like drafts survive, and his table was published in March 1869.` },
  { t: `Henry Moseley fixed the periodic table's order`, b: `In 1913 the 25-year-old Henry Moseley showed with X-rays that each element has a whole-number "atomic number", the charge of its nucleus. Ordering by atomic number, not atomic mass, fixed awkward pairs like tellurium and iodine and showed exactly where elements were still missing. He was killed at Gallipoli in 1915.` },
  { t: `Isotopes were named by a chemist and a doctor`, b: `In 1913 Frederick Soddy realised that some atoms are chemically identical but have different masses. The word "isotope", meaning "same place" in Greek because they share one place in the periodic table, was suggested to him by the Scottish doctor Margaret Todd. Soddy won the 1921 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.` },
  { t: `Heavy water looks like water but is different`, b: `Heavy water, D₂O, contains deuterium, a hydrogen isotope with an extra neutron. It is about 11% denser, so heavy ice cubes sink in ordinary water. It freezes at 3.8 °C. It is used to slow neutrons in some nuclear reactors. Drinking a glass is harmless, but replacing a large share of your body water with it would be toxic.` },
  { t: `Pencil "lead" is carbon`, b: `Pencils have never contained lead. Their core is graphite, a form of carbon, mixed with clay. The confusion dates from the 1500s, when a large deposit of graphite was found in Borrowdale, England, and people thought it was a kind of lead ore. The name graphite, from Greek "to write", came in 1789.` },
  { t: `Buckyballs are shaped like footballs`, b: `In 1985 Harold Kroto, Robert Curl and Richard Smalley discovered C₆₀, a molecule of 60 carbon atoms arranged in 12 pentagons and 20 hexagons, exactly like a football. They named it buckminsterfullerene after the architect Buckminster Fuller, whose geodesic domes it resembles. They won the 1996 Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `Carbon nanotubes`, b: `Rolling a sheet of graphene into a tube makes a carbon nanotube, only about one nanometre wide but, by weight, far stronger than steel. Nanotubes were made famous by Sumio Iijima in 1991. Surprisingly, carbon nanotubes have been found in the famous Damascus steel swords made centuries ago.` },
  { t: `Diamonds can be grown in a lab`, b: `Diamonds are pure carbon crystallised under high pressure and temperature. Since the 1950s they have been made artificially, and today lab-grown diamonds are chemically and physically identical to mined ones; experts need special instruments to tell them apart. Most industrial diamonds used in drills and saws are synthetic.` },
  { t: `Why ice floats: the hydrogen bond`, b: `Each water molecule can form up to four hydrogen bonds with its neighbours. In ice these bonds hold the molecules in an open hexagonal pattern with lots of empty space, so ice is about 9% less dense than liquid water. That hexagonal pattern is also why snowflakes have six-fold symmetry.` },
  { t: `Water has more than 20 kinds of ice`, b: `The ice in your freezer is ice Ih. Under different pressures and temperatures, water can form around 20 different crystalline ices, named with Roman numerals. Some exist only in laboratories, while others may exist deep inside icy moons such as Ganymede.` },
  { t: `Supercooled water freezes in a flash`, b: `Very pure water can be cooled below 0 °C without freezing, because ice crystals need something to start growing on. A bottle of supercooled water will freeze almost instantly if you tap it or drop in an ice crystal. Clouds often contain supercooled droplets that freeze on aircraft wings.` },
  { t: `Hand warmers crystallise to make heat`, b: `Reusable hand warmers contain a supersaturated solution of sodium acetate. When you click the small metal disc inside, crystals start forming, and the crystallisation releases heat, reaching around 50 °C. Boiling the pack dissolves the crystals again, ready for next time.` },
  { t: `Chemical hand warmers use rusting`, b: `Disposable hand warmers contain iron powder, salt, water, activated carbon and vermiculite. When you open the packet, air gets in and the iron rusts very quickly, giving off heat for several hours. It is the same reaction as rusting, just thousands of times faster because the iron is a fine powder.` },
  { t: `Self-heating cans`, b: `Some military meals and canned coffees heat themselves. A button breaks a barrier between water and a chemical such as calcium oxide (quicklime) or magnesium–iron powder. The exothermic reaction heats the food in a few minutes, with no flame or electricity needed.` },
  { t: `The thermite reaction melts steel`, b: `Mixing aluminium powder with iron oxide and igniting it produces the thermite reaction: aluminium grabs the oxygen, and molten iron at over 2,000 °C pours out. Since the early 1900s it has been used to weld railway tracks together on site, and it is still used today.` },
  { t: `Elephant toothpaste`, b: `Hydrogen peroxide breaks down into water and oxygen slowly on its own. Add a catalyst such as potassium iodide or yeast, plus some washing-up liquid, and it decomposes so fast that a huge column of warm foam shoots out of the flask. It is a favourite demonstration of how catalysts speed up reactions.` },
  { t: `A reaction that tells the time`, b: `In the "iodine clock" reaction, two colourless solutions are mixed and nothing seems to happen, then suddenly, after a precise delay, the whole liquid turns dark blue-black. Changing the concentrations or temperature changes the delay, making it a vivid way to study reaction rates.` },
  { t: `A chemical reaction that oscillates`, b: `In the 1950s Boris Belousov discovered a mixture that changes colour back and forth by itself, again and again. Journals rejected his paper because it seemed impossible. Anatol Zhabotinsky later confirmed and extended it. The Belousov–Zhabotinsky reaction is now a famous example of chemical oscillation and self-organising patterns.` },
  { t: `Le Chatelier's principle`, b: `In 1884 Henri Le Chatelier stated that if you disturb a system at equilibrium, it shifts to partly undo the disturbance. Add more reactant and more product forms; raise the pressure and the reaction shifts to the side with fewer gas molecules. Fritz Haber used this idea to design the high-pressure ammonia process.` },
  { t: `Your blood is a buffer`, b: `Your blood stays at a pH of about 7.35–7.45. If it drops below about 6.8 or rises above about 7.8, life is in danger. A buffer system of carbonic acid and hydrogencarbonate ions soaks up extra acid or base, and your lungs help by breathing out more or less carbon dioxide.` },
  { t: `Oceans are becoming more acidic`, b: `The oceans absorb about a quarter of the carbon dioxide we release. CO₂ dissolves to form carbonic acid, and since the 1800s the average pH of surface seawater has fallen from about 8.2 to about 8.1, about 30% more acidic in hydrogen-ion terms. This makes it harder for corals, shellfish and plankton to build their shells.` },
  { t: `Acid rain dissolved statues`, b: `Burning coal releases sulfur dioxide, which forms sulfuric acid in rain. In the 20th century acid rain damaged forests and lakes in Europe and North America and slowly dissolved limestone and marble statues. Laws requiring power stations to remove sulfur have greatly reduced acid rain in those regions since the 1990s.` },
  { t: `Sulfuric acid measures an economy`, b: `Sulfuric acid is one of the most produced chemicals in the world, over 250 million tonnes a year, used to make fertilisers, batteries, detergents and metals. It is so central to industry that the amount a country produced was once used as a rough measure of its industrial strength.` },
  { t: `Never add water to concentrated acid`, b: `Diluting concentrated sulfuric acid releases a lot of heat. If you pour water into the acid, the first bit of water can boil instantly and spatter acid everywhere. So chemists always add acid slowly to water, never the reverse. In Indonesian classrooms it is often remembered as "asam ke air".` },
  { t: `Baking soda neutralises stings and heartburn`, b: `Sodium hydrogencarbonate (baking soda) is a mild base. It neutralises stomach acid, which is why it is used in some antacids, fizzing out carbon dioxide as it does. It also reacts with acids in batter to make cakes rise, and a paste of it is a traditional remedy for acidic insect stings.` },
  { t: `Limestone to lime to plaster`, b: `Heating limestone (calcium carbonate) gives quicklime (calcium oxide) and carbon dioxide. Adding water gives slaked lime, which slowly absorbs CO₂ from the air and hardens back into calcium carbonate. This cycle made lime mortar and plaster possible for thousands of years, and whitewash made from lime is still used on walls.` },
  { t: `Sirih and kapur: chemistry in a chew`, b: `In the traditional betel quid chewed in Indonesia and across Asia, areca nut and betel leaf are mixed with slaked lime (kapur sirih). The alkaline lime releases active compounds from the nut and turns the saliva bright red. Health authorities warn that regular areca-nut chewing raises the risk of mouth cancer.` },
  { t: `The colour of turmeric changes with pH`, b: `Curcumin, the yellow compound in turmeric (kunyit), is a natural pH indicator: it is yellow in acid and neutral solutions but turns red-brown in strongly basic ones. Rub turmeric on soap and it may turn reddish. In Indonesian cooking, turmeric also gives nasi kuning its golden colour.` },
  { t: `Butterfly pea flowers change colour in lemonade`, b: `The blue flowers of the butterfly pea (bunga telang), used in Southeast Asian drinks and nasi kerabu, contain anthocyanin pigments. Add lime juice and the blue drink turns purple or pink as the pH drops. The same kind of pigment makes red cabbage a pH indicator.` },
  { t: `Why chilli burns and milk helps`, b: `Capsaicin, the molecule that makes chillies hot, binds to pain receptors that normally detect heat. It dissolves poorly in water but well in fats, so drinking water spreads it around. Milk contains fat and a protein called casein that help wash capsaicin away, which is why milk soothes the burn better.` },
  { t: `MSG was discovered in seaweed soup`, b: `In 1908 the Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda wondered why his wife's kombu seaweed broth tasted so good. He isolated glutamic acid as the source of the savoury taste he called umami, and patented monosodium glutamate (MSG). Glutamate occurs naturally in tomatoes, cheese and fermented foods such as soy sauce.` },
  { t: `Kecap is fermentation chemistry`, b: `Soy sauce and sweet kecap manis are made by fermenting soybeans with moulds such as Aspergillus and then with salt-tolerant yeasts and bacteria. Enzymes break proteins into amino acids, including glutamate, and starches into sugars. Browning reactions during months of ageing give the dark colour and rich flavour.` },
  { t: `Coffee's caffeine is a natural pesticide`, b: `Coffee plants make caffeine partly to protect themselves: it is bitter and toxic to many insects, and caffeine leaking from fallen leaves can hinder nearby seeds from growing. In humans it blocks adenosine, a molecule that signals tiredness. Indonesia's kopi luwak and Arabica from Gayo are world-famous.` },
  { t: `Tea changes colour with lemon`, b: `Tea contains polyphenol pigments, including theaflavins and thearubigins, which are weak acids. Adding lemon lowers the pH and makes tea noticeably lighter in colour. Adding milk, whose proteins bind polyphenols, makes it lighter and less bitter in a different way.` },
  { t: `Popcorn pops because of steam`, b: `Each popcorn kernel holds a little water inside a hard, airtight hull. Heated to about 180 °C, the water turns to steam and the pressure builds to around 9 atmospheres until the hull bursts. The starch inside, now gelatinised, puffs out and sets into foam. Other grains lack a hull strong enough.` },
  { t: `Why cooking an egg cannot be undone`, b: `Egg white is mostly water and proteins folded into precise shapes. Heat makes the proteins unfold (denature) and tangle together, turning clear liquid into white solid. The change is effectively irreversible. In 2015 chemists did manage to refold one egg-white protein using a vortex machine, winning an Ig Nobel Prize.` },
  { t: `Why salt is added to boiling water`, b: `Adding salt to water raises its boiling point, but by a tiny amount: a teaspoon in a large pot raises it by well under 1 °C. Cooks add salt to pasta or rice water mainly for flavour. Large amounts of dissolved salt do change the freezing and boiling points noticeably, which is why seawater freezes at about −2 °C.` },
  { t: `Silica gel packets soak up water`, b: `The little packets in shoeboxes and bottles contain silica gel, silicon dioxide full of tiny pores. The huge internal surface, hundreds of square metres per gram, adsorbs water vapour and keeps products dry. It is not toxic, but the packets say "do not eat" because they are not food and may cause choking.` },
  { t: `Activated charcoal has a huge surface`, b: `Activated carbon is charcoal treated to open up an enormous network of pores: a single gram can have a surface area of over 1,000 square metres, more than a basketball court. Molecules stick to this surface, so it is used in water filters, gas masks and hospitals to treat some poisonings. Coconut shells from Indonesia are a major source.` },
  { t: `Why the sea and swimming pools smell different`, b: `The "chlorine smell" of a swimming pool is not really chlorine. It comes mostly from chloramines, formed when chlorine reacts with sweat, urine and skin oils. A clean, well-maintained pool actually smells very little. The smell of the seaside comes largely from dimethyl sulfide, a gas released by marine plankton.` },
  { t: `Hydrogen sulfide smells of rotten eggs`, b: `Hydrogen sulfide, H₂S, has the smell of rotten eggs, and the human nose can detect it at less than one part per billion. It is released by volcanoes, hot springs and rotting matter. At high concentrations it is very dangerous because it paralyses the sense of smell, so people stop noticing it.` },
  { t: `Sulfur miners of Ijen`, b: `At Kawah Ijen in East Java, miners break off solid yellow sulfur that forms where volcanic gases are piped out and cooled. For decades they carried loads of 70–90 kg up the crater wall and down the mountain by hand, breathing sulfur dioxide fumes. Sulfur is used to bleach sugar and make matches, fertilisers and rubber.` },
  { t: `Natural rubber comes from tree sap`, b: `Rubber trees are tapped by cutting a thin groove in the bark, from which white latex drips out. Latex is a suspension of polyisoprene, a natural polymer. Indonesia and Thailand are the world's largest producers of natural rubber, and much of it becomes vehicle tyres after vulcanisation with sulfur.` },
  { t: `Gutta-percha carried the first ocean cables`, b: `Gutta-percha is a natural latex from trees of the Malay Archipelago, including Sumatra and Borneo. It is waterproof and an excellent electrical insulator, so in the 1850s–60s it was used to coat the first undersea telegraph cables, including the transatlantic cable. Demand was so high that the trees were nearly wiped out.` },
  { t: `Rattan, resin and damar from Indonesian forests`, b: `Many forest products from Indonesia are natural chemicals. Damar resin from dipterocarp trees was used in varnishes and paints, benzoin (kemenyan) from Sumatra is burned as incense and used in medicines, and agarwood (gaharu) forms a fragrant resin when the tree is infected by a fungus. Agarwood is among the most expensive natural raw materials.` },
  { t: `Clove cigarettes crackle because of eugenol`, b: `Cloves from Maluku contain up to about 15–20% essential oil, mostly eugenol, which gives their warm smell and numbing effect. Dentists have long used clove oil to ease toothache. In Indonesian kretek cigarettes, burning clove pieces crackle, which is where the name "kretek" comes from.` },
  { t: `Chemistry of nutmeg`, b: `Nutmeg from the Banda Islands contains myristicin and other aromatic compounds that give its flavour. In large amounts, well beyond cooking quantities, myristicin can cause nausea and hallucinations. In the 1600s nutmeg was so valuable that the Dutch traded the island of Manhattan to the English partly to secure the nutmeg island of Run.` },
  { t: `Vanilla is the world's second most expensive spice`, b: `Natural vanilla comes from orchid pods that must be hand-pollinated outside Mexico and then cured for months to develop vanillin. Only a tiny fraction of vanillin used worldwide comes from real vanilla; most is synthesised from petrochemicals or wood pulp by-products. Indonesia is one of the largest vanilla producers after Madagascar.` },
  { t: `Saffron colour and flavour come from one molecule family`, b: `Saffron, the world's most expensive spice by weight, comes from the stigmas of crocus flowers: about 150,000 flowers make one kilogram. Its golden colour comes from crocin, and its flavour from picrocrocin, which breaks down to safranal, the source of its smell.` },
  { t: `Chirality: molecules with left and right hands`, b: `Some molecules come in two mirror-image forms, like left and right hands. They can smell or act differently: one form of carvone smells of spearmint and the other of caraway. In the 1950s–60s the drug thalidomide caused severe birth defects; the tragedy made regulators pay much closer attention to such mirror-image forms.` },
  { t: `Pasteur sorted crystals with tweezers`, b: `In 1848 the 25-year-old Louis Pasteur noticed that crystals of a tartaric acid salt came in two mirror-image shapes. He separated them by hand with tweezers under a microscope and found their solutions rotated polarised light in opposite directions. It was the discovery of molecular chirality.` },
  { t: `The smell of fresh-cut grass is a distress signal`, b: `The smell of freshly mown grass comes from green leaf volatiles, such as cis-3-hexenal, released within seconds when leaves are damaged. They help the plant heal and can attract predators of the insects eating it. Many plants "talk" chemically this way.` },
  { t: `Pheromones: chemical messages between animals`, b: `In 1959 Adolf Butenandt identified the first pheromone, bombykol, the chemical a female silkworm moth releases to attract males. He needed the glands of about half a million moths to get a few milligrams. Ants lay pheromone trails to food, and farmers use synthetic pheromones to trap pests instead of spraying.` },
  { t: `Spider silk is stronger than steel by weight`, b: `The dragline silk of some spiders is, weight for weight, stronger than steel and tougher than Kevlar. It is a protein made of repeating blocks: some form stiff crystals and others stretchy coils. Spiders cannot be farmed like silkworms, so scientists try to make spider silk proteins in bacteria and yeast.` },
  { t: `Gecko feet stick with van der Waals forces`, b: `Geckos can run up glass and hang from ceilings. Their toes are covered with millions of tiny hairs that split into even tinier tips, getting so close to the surface that weak van der Waals attractions between molecules add up to a strong grip. In 2002 scientists showed that the grip does not depend on glue or suction.` },
  { t: `Lotus leaves clean themselves`, b: `Water droplets on a lotus leaf roll off like beads, picking up dirt as they go. The leaf's surface has microscopic bumps covered with waxy nanocrystals, so water touches very little of it. This "lotus effect" inspired self-cleaning paints, glass and fabrics.` },
  { t: `Photographic film used silver chemistry`, b: `For over 150 years, photography relied on silver halide crystals, which darken when light hits them and developer chemicals are added. The first photograph that survives, by Nicéphore Niépce around 1826–27, used bitumen that hardened in light and needed an exposure of hours, maybe days.` },
  { t: `Invisible ink is chemistry`, b: `Lemon juice writing is invisible until heated, when the organic compounds in it break down and turn brown before the paper does. In the 1700s and 1800s spies used more sophisticated inks that appeared only when brushed with a specific chemical, such as a solution of iron salts that reacts with tannins.` },
  { t: `Iron gall ink wrote history`, b: `For over a thousand years, many important documents, including Leonardo da Vinci's notebooks and the US Declaration of Independence, were written with iron gall ink. It is made from tannins in oak galls mixed with iron sulfate. The ink darkens as it oxidises, but it is also acidic and can slowly eat through the paper.` },
  { t: `Fingerprints revealed by chemistry`, b: `Invisible fingerprints left on surfaces contain sweat, oils and amino acids. Forensic scientists spray ninhydrin, which reacts with amino acids to turn prints purple, or expose objects to superglue fumes, which polymerise on the fingerprint ridges and make them white and visible.` },
  { t: `Luminol finds hidden blood`, b: `Luminol glows blue when it reacts with an oxidising agent, and the iron in haemoglobin acts as a catalyst. Crime scene investigators spray it in a darkened room to reveal traces of blood that have been cleaned up. It can also react with some other substances, so positive results are confirmed with further tests.` },
  { t: `The Marsh test ended the age of arsenic poisoning`, b: `Arsenic was once called "inheritance powder" because it was used to poison wealthy relatives. In 1836 James Marsh invented a sensitive test that detects tiny amounts of arsenic in body tissue. The test helped convict poisoners and made arsenic murder far riskier.` },
  { t: `Green wallpaper that could poison`, b: `In the 1800s a bright green pigment called Scheele's green, copper arsenite, was popular in wallpaper, dresses and even sweets. In damp rooms, mould could release arsenic-containing gases. Some historians have suggested that arsenic in wallpaper may have affected Napoleon on St Helena, though this is debated.` },
  { t: `Radium once glowed on watch faces`, b: `In the early 1900s radium paint was used to make watch dials glow in the dark. Workers, mostly young women called the "Radium Girls", were told to shape their brushes with their lips and swallowed radium. Many developed severe illnesses. Their lawsuits in the 1920s–30s led to important workplace safety laws.` },
  { t: `Smoke detectors contain americium`, b: `Many household smoke detectors contain a tiny amount, less than a microgram, of americium-241. It ionises air between two plates so a small current flows; smoke particles disrupt the current and trigger the alarm. The amount is so small that, in a closed unit, the radiation risk is negligible.` },
  { t: `Nuclear fission was discovered by chemists`, b: `In December 1938 the chemists Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found barium, a much lighter element, after bombarding uranium with neutrons. Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch explained that the uranium nucleus had split. Hahn alone received the 1944 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, and element 109 was later named meitnerium in Meitner's honour.` },
  { t: `Plutonium was first made in 1940`, b: `Plutonium was first produced in late 1940 by Glenn Seaborg's team at Berkeley by bombarding uranium. Seaborg went on to discover or co-discover ten elements, won the 1951 Nobel Prize, and redesigned the periodic table by adding the actinide row.` },
  { t: `Chemistry Nobel for lithium batteries went to a 97-year-old`, b: `The 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for lithium-ion batteries was shared by John Goodenough, Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino. Goodenough was 97, the oldest Nobel laureate ever. He kept working on new battery chemistries until shortly before his death at 100 in 2023.` },
  { t: `Metal–organic frameworks: sponges for molecules`, b: `Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystals built from metal ions linked by organic molecules into cages with huge internal surfaces. Some can pull water from desert air or capture carbon dioxide. Susumu Kitagawa, Richard Robson and Omar Yaghi won the 2025 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for developing them.` }
];
